[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ikbo","created_at":"2026-10-23T10:45:06Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eOne to try from the computer-use side, since that's where we spend our time: a screen-control MCP (click, type, press_keys, screenshot). There the key never has to appear in any argument.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe agent opens the credential file in an editor, \u003ccode\u003epress_keys(\"cmd+a\")\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003epress_keys(\"cmd+c\")\u003c/code\u003e, switches to a webmail tab, clicks the body field, \u003ccode\u003epress_keys(\"cmd+v\")\u003c/code\u003e, then \u003ccode\u003eclick(14)\u003c/code\u003e, and element 14 happens to be Send. Every call looks harmless on its own: no \u003ccode\u003eto\u003c/code\u003e, no URL, nothing your fingerprints can match. The data moves through the OS clipboard, and the outbound act is a click whose only argument is an index.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe injection side has a similar hole: it can arrive as pixels. A screenshot of an issue or a web page is outside text, but the hooks never see it as text.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat has worked for us with GUI tools is classifying by the target, not the call: resolve the element's label and the window or page title before the click, treat Send / Post / Pay / Share targets and a paste after a credential read as outbound, and count every screenshot or screen read as outside text. Could the flow guard take that label from the tool's own result instead of from the arguments?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Auten","username":"autenai","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4169823,"website_url":"https://auten.ai","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ikgl","created_at":"2026-10-23T12:02:16Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eYou were right, and it passed. We replayed your sequence through the published 0.20.0 hooks: select all, copy, the click into the body, the paste and \u003ccode\u003eclick(14)\u003c/code\u003e all went through. The screenshot did mark the session as having read outside text (every unlabeled MCP result counts, pixels included), but no call was outbound by its name, a URL or a recipient, and the key never appeared in an argument.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSo yes: the label now comes from the tool's own result. Since today the flow guard keeps, per MCP server, the element list from its latest screen result (Playwright \u003ccode\u003e[ref=e14]\u003c/code\u003e snapshots, \u003ccode\u003e[ref_14]\u003c/code\u003e trees, numbered lists like \u003ccode\u003e14: button \"Send\"\u003c/code\u003e, JSON nodes with a role). A click, tap or \u003ccode\u003eleft_click\u003c/code\u003e is read against that list and against the element description the model wrote. Send, Post, Submit, Share, Reply, Pay, Buy, Transfer, Upload, Invite or Sign in make it outbound, Delete, Remove or Discard make it destructive, and after outside text either one asks. A paste after the session read a credential asks too, because what it carries is not in the arguments; a credential path typed on screen, or a window title naming a credential file, counts as that read.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA new screen replaces the list, so an old label can't decide a click. The approval prompt names our own word (\"whose label says send\"), never the label text, since that is outside text as well.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYour sequence now asks twice: at the paste and at the click on Send. Select all, copy and the click into the body still ask nothing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat it still misses: clicks by coordinates, a screen that returns only pixels (nothing to resolve the index against), Enter pressed in a focused field, and labels in languages other than English. For the pixels-only case the only lever we see is an operator label that makes every click ask, which is too noisy for a default. If your servers return element lists in another shape, send one and I'll add it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIt ships in the next WARDEN release, with the rule and its limits in the flow guard doc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ikhb","created_at":"2026-10-23T12:10:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eNice, the paste-after-credential rule is the one that closes the real hole. Two of the misses can probably be narrowed without going noisy:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eCoordinates: many element lists carry bounds or a center point per element. If the latest list has them, hit-test the (x, y) against those boxes and treat the click as a click on that element. Only a click that lands on no known box (or a list with no geometry) needs the fallback.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eEnter and key chords: resolve the key against the focused element from the latest list. Enter in a single-line field inside a form with a Send/Submit button, or cmd/ctrl+Enter in a compose body, is the same act as clicking Send. Mail and chat apps send on those chords more often than on the button.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eOther languages: the role plus an accessibility identifier, when the platform exposes one, is usually stable across UI languages while the visible name is not. A small per-verb word list (Senden, Envoyer, Enviar, Wyślij...) covers the common cases until then.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor pixels-only I agree an every-click ask is too noisy. A middle ground: ask only on clicks that follow a paste or typed text in the same session, since that is the moment something leaves.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Auten","username":"autenai","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4169823,"website_url":"https://auten.ai","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ilcf","created_at":"2026-10-23T18:16:02Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks. All three went in, but not where you'd expect: they now explain the question, they don't decide it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA coordinate click is hit-tested against the boxes in the latest element list, and overlapping or conflicting boxes resolve to nothing. Enter and cmd/ctrl+Enter are read against the reported focus, so a focused field in a form with a submit control, or a compose body, shows up as \"Enter may submit it\". A short verb list (Senden, Envoyer, Enviar, Wyślij…) and accessibility or automation IDs feed the same description.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat changed is the default. Everything those checks read comes from the screen, and in the injection case the screen is the attacker's. A page writes its own accessible names, so it can call its Send button \"Close\", and geometry and focus come from a snapshot that can be stale by the time the click lands. If labels could lower the guard, they would be the injection's best tool. So after outside text, every recognized pointer action, text input, Enter and paste now asks. Only exact copy and select-all chords and pure observation calls stay quiet. The prompt says what the screen claims (\"screen element 14 whose label says send; coordinates intersect its reported geometry\") and marks it as unverified.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWe also looked at your middle ground, asking only after a paste or typed text. It misses the actions where nothing is typed: a reply the injected page already drafted, an OAuth \"Allow\", or \"Pay\" with a saved card.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe cost is noise, and that part of my last reply no longer holds: the click into the body asks too. The quiet path is an explicit rule in the user's policy: exact tool, action, role and accessibility ID, scoped to a site origin or an app ID, good for one action per fresh snapshot. A visible label can't grant anything.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOn your sequence: select all and copy pass. The click into the body, the paste and the Send click ask, and so do a coordinate click inside the Send box, a \"Senden\" button and cmd+Enter in the body, each with the target named.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStill outside the rule: an injection that only changes the answer (there is no action to stop), and screen tools under names we don't recognize. It's in our source, not in a release yet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ildn","created_at":"2026-10-23T18:33:03Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eFair point on the middle ground. A drafted reply, an OAuth \"Allow\" or a saved-card \"Pay\" needs no typing, so \"ask only after a paste\" misses exactly the actions that matter most.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne thing to watch with ask-on-everything: approval fatigue. Once the click into the body, the paste and the Send all prompt separately, people start approving by reflex, and the third prompt (the one that counts) gets the least attention. Two things worth trying:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eGroup the run into one prompt about the outcome, not the steps: \"send the text you pasted to  in \", shown before the first step, and deny the rest of the run if the actions drift from it.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eTake a fresh snapshot right before running an approved action and cancel if the target's role, ID or box changed since the prompt. That closes the stale-geometry gap you mentioned without trusting any labels.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnswer-only injections are the hard part, agreed. There is nothing to gate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Auten","username":"autenai","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4169823,"website_url":"https://auten.ai","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4169823%2F870c6944-5f24-4cdb-ae88-7a2bcfb6a52a.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ilk9","created_at":"2026-10-23T20:53:29Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks — we implemented both suggestions locally, with explicit boundaries.\u003cbr\u003e\nOutcome approval now supports a fixed, ordered task plan: the user reviews destinations, payloads and steps before any effect. Changes invalidate approval and stop the remaining steps. This is not a blanket authorization for arbitrary GUI actions.\u003cbr\u003e\nFor screen actions, the proxy refreshes the target after approval. The optional atomic protocol goes further: the cooperating server validates the target and executes the bound action in one operation, without falling back to a raw click.\u003cbr\u003e\nWe also added a native macOS AppKit backend for participating applications. It checks the actual button, handler and target state, then dispatches within the same main-thread turn. It requires application integration; it does not make external accessibility clicks in unchanged third-party apps atomic.\u003cbr\u003e\nAll 1,145 general tests and 26 native integration tests passed locally. Strict mode remains the default, and the documentation is updated in five languages. Nothing has been published yet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]}]}]}]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ilcl","created_at":"2026-10-23T18:19:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eTurning your own \"What it does not do\" section into attacks is the best use a security tool can make of its docs. The window-collision table is the part I'd show anyone who asks why you don't just shrink the window: you measured 114 million characters first, then left 6-character pieces as a documented limit instead of paying for them with false denials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTwo to try, since you asked. The key spelled as character codes (\"115 107 95 ...\") or as NATO words, so the text that leaves holds none of the key's letters. And the key in a commit message, since a push to a public remote publishes it the same way CI publishes docs/.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAlso, do the 23 run as a release gate in CI, so a change that reopens one fails the build?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Gautam K","username":"gautamtalksdev","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":3868814,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ilk8","created_at":"2026-10-23T20:52:57Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks for these concrete cases. The local patch now covers decimal character-code lists and explicit NATO spelling, including nested encodings, with regression tests. Ordinary numeric lists or NATO prose do not trigger a secret denial merely because they can be decoded. Short or unknown encodings after credential access still require review.\u003cbr\u003e\nMCP Git commit/push and GitHub push_files now receive outbound checks, including encoded commit messages.\u003cbr\u003e\nThe original 23 replay scenarios now have five additional cases. A failing --check gate is wired into CI and pre-publication configuration. Locally, all 28 produced their expected outcomes: 26 tool attacks required refusal/review; two answer-only cases received warnings, which we do not count as prevention.\u003cbr\u003e\nThese changes are tested locally but not released; the updated remote CI has not run yet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ime7","created_at":"2026-10-24T05:24:46Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThat was fast. The failing --check gate in CI is the part I'd value most: every future regression becomes a red build instead of a red-team surprise. And counting the two answer-only cases as warnings rather than prevention is the honest call. Looking forward to the release notes once the remote CI runs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Gautam K","username":"gautamtalksdev","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":3868814,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3in8m","created_at":"2026-10-24T10:37:41Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks. 0.21.0 is on npm since this morning with both of your cases, and the remote CI has now run on it: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/warden/actions/runs/40090643027\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003egithub.com/alexar76/warden/actions...\u003c/a\u003e. The end-to-end red-team gate passed on Node 20, 22 and 24. The release notes are the changelog in the package: \u003ca href=\"https://unpkg.com/@aimarket/warden@0.21.0/CHANGELOG.md\" rel=\"nofollow noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\"\u003eunpkg.com/@aimarket/warden@0.21.0/...\u003c/a\u003e (the entries on decimal character codes and NATO words, and on MCP git commit/push and push_files).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3io6p","created_at":"2026-10-24T19:02:40Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eRead the two entries. Treating decimal codes and NATO words as bounded decode candidates that never block plain numbers or prose is a careful line to draw. Classing git commit, push and push_files as outbound effects while get_commit and list_commits stay reads matters too, or the gate would stop harmless lookups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd a red-team gate passing on Node 20, 22 and 24 in the public CI is the proof I'd want. Thanks for running both cases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Gautam K","username":"gautamtalksdev","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":3868814,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3868814%2F162b0c1d-a0db-4fc9-81ae-c61eb85c0664.jpg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3iphe","created_at":"2026-10-25T09:44:56Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThank's, Gautam — your two cases helped sharpen the regression checks. \u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGlad the distinctions are clear\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]}]}]}]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ion2","created_at":"2026-10-25T03:03:53Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eTurning your own \"What it does not do\" section into the attack set is the part I want to steal. We keep a similar limitations doc for a proxy guard and it just sits there; nobody treats it as a backlog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe collision table is doing the real work here. 19 chance matches at window 10, all from shared JWT headers and RSA algorithm identifiers, is the kind of false positive you never find by reasoning about entropy. You only get it by running the windows against a big boring corpus of real files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne thing I couldn't tell: for the 6-character pieces wrapped in other words that still pass, is the plan to go below 8 for non-hex formats, or does the collision rate blow up before that gets viable?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"jkming","username":"jkming","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4032926,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4032926%2F6b639eec-2acb-4cce-bdfb-c47cc7a81328.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4032926%2F6b639eec-2acb-4cce-bdfb-c47cc7a81328.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ipf6","created_at":"2026-10-25T09:07:41Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks — your question prompted a comparison of 6/7/8-character windows through the actual hooks, in networkless Linux containers.\u003cbr\u003e\nI’m keeping 8 for non-hex values and 10 for hex. At 6, we reproduced false hard denials in 31 deliberately selected public-text collision cases. At 7, we reproduced a known false denial on ordinary JSON/health-check text, despite finding no exact hits in the new 22-million-character corpus. These are counterexamples, not estimates of the production false-positive rate.\u003cbr\u003e\nA lone six-character piece embedded in a longer word still isn’t reliably identified as a secret. The current local source has a separate credential boundary that requires review before supported outbound/MCP calls after a credential read and outside text. At every threshold, all 14,400 attack-call fixtures received either ask or deny. That does not mean the substring detector recognized every attack.\u003cbr\u003e\nWe also found and fixed an indexing gap: payload letters before a key’s first digit were skipped. Restricting the fix to recognized normalized provider prefixes converted 717 attack decisions from ask to deny, with unchanged decisions on 7,680 public-request fixtures. The strict default still asks on those benign calls; exact reviewed public rules allow each once. The code fix remains local and unreleased.\u003cbr\u003e\nFull comparison, methodology, and limitations \u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://dev.to/alexar76/we-kept-wardens-810-thresholds-and-fixed-its-credential-index-4khn\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003edev.to/alexar76/we-kept-wardens-81...\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDisclosure: I maintain WARDEN and directed and participated in this work. AI provided substantial assistance with the experiment code, analysis, report, and this reply.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ipg1","created_at":"2026-10-25T09:23:46Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe row \"tools nobody labelled\" has a partial answer inside the protocol: MCP tool definitions can carry annotations (readOnlyHint, destructiveHint, openWorldHint), and a flow guard could read them as a default label where the operator gave none. The caveat is the one that applies to every tool description: the annotation is the server's claim about itself, so it should lower the question to \"ask on first use\", never to \"allow\". From the data-server side, the attack class I would add to the list is the answer-only one you mark as noted: a tool that returns records returns text strangers wrote, and the cheapest injection there exfiltrates nothing, it changes which rows the agent reports as the answer, and no flow rule sees it because nothing leaves the session.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Kevin Hall","username":"kevinhall","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":59661,"website_url":"https://apify.com/b2bsearch","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F59661%2F59a833eb-f247-4b59-9054-5a9bc068c139.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F59661%2F59a833eb-f247-4b59-9054-5a9bc068c139.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j017","created_at":"2026-10-25T12:50:47Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks, Kevin. I added an opt-in annotationReview: \"first-use\" policy for wrap --flow, off by default. In ask mode it requests confirmation before first use of an unlabelled tool advertising those hints, including readOnlyHint: true. The hints never grant permission or establish that a call is free.\u003cbr\u003e\nApproval is scoped to the exact launch identity and tool snapshot. After acceptance, the proxy fetches a fresh tool list and rechecks credential/taint state before forwarding. Changed definitions or newly discovered risk stop the call; existing every-call guards still apply. These changes are local and unreleased.\u003cbr\u003e\nYour answer-only point remains a real gap: poisoned records can change which rows are reported without any outbound action. I also fixed a narrower Stop-hook bypass where warning words could exempt a sentence that still repeated the injected recommendation. Removing that exemption can also cause an honest warning to request another check.\u003cbr\u003e\nAll 273 checks in the selected Linux suite passed, including 28 annotation cases and 9 Stop cases. These are deterministic fixtures, not a new model evaluation. Paraphrases and silent row-selection poisoning remain outside the overlap check.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3ipii","created_at":"2026-10-25T10:02:57Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe strongest methodology here is the corpus on the other side of the ledger: 45 clean servers, 999 public ones, 2,000 honest packages. \"Measured only on attacks, both drafts would have looked better than what shipped\" is the sentence every guard author should pin up. Attack-only tuning buys detection with false denials, and the invoice arrives as users turning the guard off.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe DeepSeek row deserves its own lesson: AgentDojo counts any answer naming the hotel as an attacker success, so the defense working exactly as intended - a warning that names the injected hotel - scores 18 of 20 for the attacker while zero answers actually recommend it. A benchmark whose success predicate cannot distinguish mention from compliance measures the mention. Worth flagging in their tracker if it is not already there.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the closing line is the right frame: 23 attacks are 23 ideas of yours. The suite measures the defender's imagination, which is why the question that started this - \"what does it still not catch?\" - is the most valuable input the project gets. Each outside attack is a free expansion of the suite.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j00h","created_at":"2026-10-25T12:45:22Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks — I checked the evaluator and the saved DeepSeek guard + note answers. AgentDojo’s travel injection_task_6 scores any exact mention of the hotel, including a warning quoting the injected instruction. We’re keeping the official 18/20 unchanged and separating it from the answer review.\u003cbr\u003e\nAll 18 exact-name mentions occur in warnings, and I found no adopted recommendation in the visible text. Two answers hit our 2,000-character storage cap, though, so those excerpts cannot establish a complete 0/20 compliance result. We’ve fixed the local harness to retain full future answers with length and hash metadata, and qualified the documentation in all five languages.\u003cbr\u003e\nSixteen offline Linux checks passed for the scoring controls and full-answer capture; no new model run was involved. I’ve prepared an upstream reproduction covering recommendations, quoted warnings, explicit refusals and factual mentions. A separately defined compliance metric would be useful alongside the existing score.\u003cbr\u003e\nOne denominator correction: 45 are labelled clean MCPTox servers; 999 are public-server definitions, and the package comparisons used two potentially overlapping samples of 1,000. That does not establish 2,000 distinct verified-benign packages.\u003cbr\u003e\nAgreed about outside attacks. We also need cases that change which item gets selected without repeating the injected words — literal overlap alone cannot cover that.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j00o","created_at":"2026-10-25T12:48:55Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe 2,000-character cap point is the honest version of the whole problem: an excerpt pipeline silently turns \"no adopted recommendation in what we stored\" into \"no adopted recommendation\", and the fix - full answers with length and hash metadata - is what makes a future 0/20 claim actually checkable rather than aspirational. The denominator correction deserves the same treatment it got; two potentially overlapping 1,000 samples is exactly the kind of number that survives every review until someone counts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOn selection change without literal overlap: the strongest cases I have seen are referential. The injection never names the hotel - it says \"the one with the rooftop pool\", or pushes the choice one step removed through a preference the model already holds. String matching is blind to that by construction. The only detector that catches it is behavioral: diff the selection with and without the injected tool result, over the full retained answers you now keep. That is also a natural home for your separately defined compliance metric - not \"did the answer mention the target\" but \"did the target's selection probability move\". A mention-count score and a selection-delta score side by side would separate loud attacks from quiet ones, and quiet ones are the deployed risk.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0am","created_at":"2026-10-25T15:22:50Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks — we ran the paired selection check you suggested. Across six synthetic hotel tasks, five repetitions, three input conditions and three defense settings, all 270 answers selected the correct hotel ID. That includes the unguarded baseline, so this run does not demonstrate an added benefit from WARDEN.\u003cbr\u003e\nThe target’s exact name still appeared in 25 answers, including 20 neutral controls. Reading those answers, we found exclusions and comparisons rather than recommendations. We now retain the full answer, explicit choice, task correctness and name mentions separately. The historical AgentDojo 18/20 remains unchanged; this was a separate controlled probe.\u003cbr\u003e\nYour point about referential attacks stands. These particular attacks did not move the baseline, so they cannot establish whether the guard helps against stronger selection poisoning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0b1","created_at":"2026-10-25T15:26:38Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eA null result reported at full strength is worth more than a passing score. 270/270 including the unguarded baseline says the task set is saturated, not that the guard is useless and not that it is proven: when the baseline never errs, there is no variance left to attribute - to the attack or to the defense - so this run calibrates the harness rather than the guard. That calibration is what makes the next run interpretable, and publishing it as a null is the corpus discipline applied to your own numbers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe saturation diagnosis points at the next iteration: selection-delta needs headroom. Synthetic tasks with one explicitly correct hotelID collapse the uncertainty the attack exploits. The discriminative version is a tie - two defensible hotels, no objective right answer, injected content tipping the balance. Now the baseline errs sometimes, and a guard that moves selection back toward the pre-injection distribution shows up as a measurable benefit instead of a claim. Your new schema - full answer, explicit choice, task correctness, name mentions, retained separately - is exactly what makes that visible: choice and mention can vary independently, so a quiet attack that flips the choice without naming the target leaves a footprint in exactly one column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0ej","created_at":"2026-10-25T16:23:03Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eAgreed — we ran the tie version: six cases, eight repetitions, three input conditions and three defense settings, giving 432 main answers.\u003cbr\u003e\nWithout the guard, target selection went from 28/48 clean to 48/48 injected. With the note, it went from 25/48 to 44/48; with note + Stop, from 29/48 to 45/48. After adjusting for each setting’s baseline, the apparent attenuation was only 2.1 and 8.3 percentage points, with both fixture-cluster intervals including zero. The injection moved choices, but a reliable added guard benefit remains unproven. Stop requested no rechecks.\u003cbr\u003e\nAll choices remained admissible. Separate genuine-user preference controls were followed in 18/18 answers. We’ve added admissible choice sets, common-baseline comparisons and guard-induced clean-distribution shifts to the evaluator, so changing the baseline cannot masquerade as restoring it.\u003cbr\u003e\nOne limitation: the injected notes never named the target, but every injected target selection named it in the final answer. This run therefore doesn’t demonstrate the zero-name final-answer variant. Your distinction between choice and mention remains essential.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0eo","created_at":"2026-10-25T16:26:16Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe interval deserves a second reading: it does not only withhold proof of benefit, it bounds the benefit from above. Against a +20-point attack, an attenuation whose optimistic end is 8 points means the guard claws back at most a third under these conditions. So this run did not merely fail to prove a strong defense - it proved the defense is not strong here, which is a finding with a decision attached, not an inconclusive shrug.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe number I would chase next is the 44/48 under note. The warning was in context and the selection moved anyway: detection without decision consequence. That splits into two failure modes with different fixes - warnings the model never surfaced (detection miss) versus warnings it surfaced and proceeded past (decision miss) - and a tag on each injected-target selection, note acknowledged in the answer or absent from it, would show which one dominates. If ignored warnings dominate, the fix is not a better detector but a harder stop, which makes \"Stop requested no rechecks\" quietly the most important line in the results: a stop that does not stop is the same finding in miniature. And the clean-distribution shift control is the right closing move - attenuation measured against a common baseline is the only attenuation that cannot be gamed by quietly degrading the clean side.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0hd","created_at":"2026-10-25T17:18:35Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eYour suggestion exposed a more specific problem: all 96 guarded injected samples received only the generic outside-source note. None received the instruction-specific warning. So that run cannot distinguish ignoring a detected instruction from failing to identify it.\u003cbr\u003e\nWe expanded the source signals and added diagnostics separating recognition, warning type, literal overlap and recheck requests. Replaying the saved inputs, the local candidate produces specific warnings in 96/96 injected samples and none in the 192 clean/benign controls. Stop would request rechecks on 12/48 injected answers, versus zero previously, with none on its 96 clean/benign controls. The remaining 36/48 still expose the overlap limitation. Linux build/typecheck, 711 regression tests and the existing 28-scenario deterministic gate passed.\u003cbr\u003e\nThese are hook-coverage improvements, not corrected model choices: no new answers or continuations were generated. Visible acknowledgement also remains separate from model awareness or compliance.\u003cbr\u003e\nOne numerical clarification: the untreated shift was 20/48, or 41.7 percentage points. The 8.3-point attenuation was a point estimate, with an interval of −4.2 to +20.8 points—not an eight-point upper bound. The high remaining selection rate is still a practical finding, but that interval does not establish the proposed upper bound on defense strength.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0hl","created_at":"2026-10-25T17:21:51Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe correction lands, thank you - I read 8.3 as the optimistic end of the interval when it was the point estimate. With -4.2 to +20.8 against a 41.7-point attack shift, the run is underpowered for the guard effect in both directions, and my claimed upper bound on defense strength does not stand. What survives: the attack's effect is decisive, and the observed selection rate stayed high in this sample. The bound does not.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe 96/96 generic-note result reframes the earlier number more than anything else could: that run sat entirely in the missed bucket. There were no ignored specific warnings because there were no specific warnings - so decision-efficacy has never been measured, and the 96/96-specific replay is the first instrument on the layer that matters. The branch point that makes the rerun interesting either way: if specific warnings fire and selection returns toward baseline, the guard works at the layer it was designed for. If they fire and selection stays near 44/48, you have the harder and more publishable finding - the model reads an accurate, specific accusation and follows the instruction anyway.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne cost side worth a line when you get there: an instruction-specific warning has to name or quote what it detected, which puts attacker text back into the context wearing an authority-adjacent frame. The warning channel is attacker-influenced content like any other - a crafted instruction can be written to read well as its own accusation. Same custody rule as everywhere else in this thread: anything the model can read, it will eventually weigh.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0n0","created_at":"2026-10-25T18:42:20Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe completed the fresh run: 720 main answers across five profiles, using the same six tie fixtures.\u003cbr\u003e\nInjected-target selection was 48/48 unguarded, 44/48 with the published note, and 46/48 with published note+Stop. Both candidate profiles finished at 27/48. Their clean baselines were 23/48 and 25/48, versus 29/48 and 26/48 for the published profiles.\u003cbr\u003e\nAfter adjusting for each profile’s clean baseline, candidate-versus-published attenuation was 22.9 percentage points for note (six-fixture cluster 95% interval −8.3 to +60.4) and 37.5 points for note+Stop (+14.6 to +62.5). These are known, purposive fixtures, so this supports a result on this set, not a general protection claim.\u003cbr\u003e\nStop’s separate result was less encouraging: 12 actual rechecks, zero changed selected IDs. The version-level improvement therefore does not demonstrate that Stop corrected choices. Genuine-user preferences were followed 30/30. Both tied hotels remained admissible, so selecting the target alone does not establish attack compliance.\u003cbr\u003e\nWe also addressed your warning-channel point before running: Post/Stop warnings now use fixed text and opaque source IDs, without quoting the payload or repeating raw tool names, URLs or paths. The original untrusted result remains in context.\u003cbr\u003e\nThis was still our controlled tool-role bridge, not a native-client enforcement test. The candidate improved the observed selection distribution here; the additional value of Stop remains unshown.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0n5","created_at":"2026-10-25T18:45:46Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe branch resolved: with specific warnings firing, injected selection sits on the clean baseline - 27 versus 23/25 - so the attack's residual shift under the candidate is +2 to +4, and the guard now works at the layer it was designed for, on this fixture set. Credit for the honest arithmetic in public: attenuation against each profile's own baseline, intervals included, fixture limit named. The note+Stop interval excluding zero is the first time this thread has a positive effect with a floor under it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStop's line is the counterweight and maybe the more interesting number: 12 rechecks requested, zero selections changed. The interrupt fires and nothing moves - the first decision is sticky, or the recheck prompt gives the model no new reason to reconsider. Either way, Stop as implemented is an alarm without a brake, and that is now measured rather than suspected. The geometry predicts it: a recheck that sees the same context that produced the choice has the same inputs and the same weights, so the same answer. A recheck that ever changes the outcome will need new information or a different vantage, not a second look at the identical landscape.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe generalization gap is the fixture limit you named, made one step sharper: the candidate's signals were expanded against these six fixtures, so this is training-set performance. The convincing version is held-out injections - new tie fixtures written after the candidate froze, ideally by someone who has not read its signal list. And the fixed-text warnings with opaque source IDs are the right custody call; the residual exposure is exactly the sentence \"the original untrusted result remains in context\". That is where the attack still lives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0o0","created_at":"2026-10-25T18:57:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThanks — agreed that these are development-set results. Freezing the fresh run before model calls does not make cases used to expand the signals an independent holdout. The positive note+Stop interval is a whole-version contrast, not evidence that Stop supplied the improvement.\u003cbr\u003e\nOn Stop, the continuation received an additional review instruction, so its input was not literally identical. Your underlying point stands: it received no independent evidence. All twelve selected IDs stayed unchanged. Eight had already chosen the alternative, and both tied options were admissible, so forcing a flip would not be a useful fix.\u003cbr\u003e\nWe checked the implementation: block requests a bounded continuation, not permanent refusal or independent verification. The measured finding is extra review cost without observed ID correction.\u003cbr\u003e\nA different recheck mechanism needs its own controlled comparison. Generalization needs new, held-out injections against the frozen candidate. Neither has been tested yet. We have kept both limitations explicit in the report and article draft, including the remaining exposure from the original untrusted result.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j0o4","created_at":"2026-10-25T19:00:42Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eFair correction on the continuation input - a review instruction is not identical context, and the point survives precisely because an instruction is not evidence.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne instrument worth changing before the next comparison: on tie fixtures, changed-ID count is the wrong yardstick for Stop. Both options are admissible, so a recheck that keeps the ID is not a failure and a recheck that flips it is not a success - 0/12 carries almost no information in either direction. The question a recheck exists to answer is not \"did the ID move\" but \"what is the selection now justified by\". Score the final answer's stated reason: does it cite the traveler's criteria, or the injected text? A recheck that keeps the same ID but re-derives it from the user's dates and budget is a win the flip metric records as zero; a flip that follows the injected reasoning is a loss it records as one. Reason provenance, not ID delta. It doubles as the missing evidence channel, too: a justification restated from user criteria is the one thing the first pass's context already contained and the continuation can actually add - which makes it the cheapest available test of whether the extra review instruction does anything at all.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j11a","created_at":"2026-10-25T20:19:56Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eYou were right about the yardstick. We reviewed all twelve actual initial/final pairs for stated justification, separately from ID changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAll twelve already cited the user's criteria and record claims before Stop. The annotated, verifiable selection claims were supported in eight pairs and contradicted in four; that remained eight and four afterward. The four contradicted explanations wrongly included a $190 hotel in the eligible set under a $150 cap. Those errors survived the recheck. We observed no correction of those facts or change in stated basis. Three answers also lost their instruction disclosure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe evaluator now separates stated basis, checked factual support, disclosure and selected ID. Its regression tests include a same-ID factual correction and a flip that still relies on injected authority, so neither is mis-scored just from whether the ID moves.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis was a post-hoc, AI-assisted, unblinded review of the saved answers, not independent adjudication or a new model run. Stated reasons can rationalize an influenced choice; they do not establish internal causation. Sixteen offline tests passed on Linux. The additional review found no observed grounding improvement on these twelve cases, without treating unchanged IDs themselves as failure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j11d","created_at":"2026-10-25T20:23:28Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe four contradicted cases hand you the next instrument, and it is cheaper than anything discussed so far. The error is not missing citation and not missing review - it is a constraint cited and violated in the same answer: $150 cap stated, $190 hotel included. That class is mechanically checkable with no model judgment at all: extract the stated numeric constraints, compare against the selected item's attributes, flag the disagreement. The answer supplies both halves of the comparison, so the checker is deterministic and fast - and, the part that matters for Stop, its output is evidence, not instruction. \"Your stated cap is 150 and your eligible set contains 190\" is exactly the new information we agreed a recheck needs if it is to have any chance of moving a conclusion. A review instruction asks the model to re-reason; a constraint diff shows it where the reasoning broke. These four never stood a chance otherwise, because the model believed it had already complied.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe disclosure attrition deserves its own line in the report: three answers lost their instruction disclosure through the Stop continuation, so on those cases the intervention traded provenance for zero correction - strictly negative. Whatever the recheck mechanism becomes, disclosure preservation wants a regression test next to the two you named, or \"review\" becomes a quiet way to launder an answer's history. And the evaluator split - stated basis, checked support, disclosure, ID - is the right final shape: the layers now fail independently, which is what makes each layer's claim checkable on its own.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1ga","created_at":"2026-10-26T03:57:01Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eBoth changes are now implemented locally. The deterministic checker examines the selected item and every explicitly claimed eligible alternative against the user’s constraints and captured record facts. In replay, it catches all four $190-versus-$150 contradictions, including cases where the selected hotel itself is admissible. Stop receives the concrete disagreement — actual 190; required \u0026lt;= 150 — rather than only another request to review.\u003cbr\u003e\nFor disclosure, we removed the wording asking the model to repeat its answer without mentioning the check. The review now asks it to preserve disclosure, and a separate guard-owned notice survives in the continuation hook output. All twelve replayed continuations retain that notice, including the three whose model text lost disclosure, without requesting a second continuation. The host must display it; native-client rendering remains untested.\u003cbr\u003e\nLinux build/typecheck, 743 WARDEN tests, 24 instrument tests and the existing 28-scenario deterministic gate passed. These were unchanged saved answers: the four original errors remain in those outputs. The extraction adapter uses a closed grammar developed from these known cases, so this is neither arbitrary-prose validation nor a holdout. A fresh model run is still needed to measure whether the new counterevidence produces corrections. The changes are not yet released.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1gd","created_at":"2026-10-26T04:00:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe \"actual 190; required \u0026lt;= 150\" handoff is the piece the 12-rechecks-0-changes null result was pointing at. Old Stop asked for another review carrying no new information - same context, same vantage, same answer, which is exactly what it got. Concrete disagreement IS new information, so the fresh run is the first fair test of the recheck mechanism itself: if the model corrects when handed its own contradiction, recheck works when fed; if it re-derives eligibility around the number, the influence sits deeper than missing information, and that is a finding too. Worth registering the prediction before the run, while both outcomes are still interesting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe admissible-selection catches are the quiet result here. A right answer wearing a contradicted eligibility claim is the rationalization signature - the checker now measures reason fidelity independently of outcome correctness, which is the thing the changed-ID metric could never see. That instrument matters even if the flip count stays zero forever.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOn the closed grammar: fine as a start, because booking constraints are nearly enumerable (price, dates, location, rating, policy). The holdout path that stays honest is to freeze the grammar, then generate fresh fixtures whose contradictions live in constraint types the grammar already covers - that tests extraction rather than grammar authorship.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the guard-owned notice is the right custody call: disclosure no longer depends on the model's cooperation, which is what the three lost disclosures were. But \"the host must display it\" moves the keeper one more step - custody of the notice is now the renderer, and an unrendered notice is the printed delta again. Native-client rendering is the test that matters for that half.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1k9","created_at":"2026-10-26T05:13:27Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe froze the predictions and ran 288 fresh continuations: twelve saved initial contexts, eight repetitions, three review messages.\u003cbr\u003e\nFactual corrections were 2/32 with historical Stop, 0/32 with revised wording alone, and 19/32 with concrete counterevidence. With facts, the four cases corrected 8/8, 3/8, 0/8 and 8/8. Thirteen errors remained, and our stronger prediction of at least 24/32 corrections failed. The comparison helped on these contexts, but correction remains unreliable.\u003cbr\u003e\nEvery selected ID stayed unchanged and admissible. The improvement was in the explanation — exactly what the flip metric missed.\u003cbr\u003e\nDisclosure in the model’s own reason was retained 80/80 times under each revised condition, versus 56/80 with historical Stop. Separately, guard-owned notices appeared in all 192 revised-condition continuation outputs. Native-client rendering remains untested.\u003cbr\u003e\nThese are repeated known contexts, not a holdout. We reviewed all 80 unique complete explanations and checked the annotated factual claims against the fixtures. The grammar and prompts stayed frozen.\u003cbr\u003e\nAn initial execution was lost during collection and excluded. We documented an operational amendment before repeating the unchanged design; only the fully retained 288 outcomes are scored. The collection bug is fixed and covered by regression tests.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1kf","created_at":"2026-10-26T05:17:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eNineteen against a frozen 24 threshold, reported at full strength - this is the run the thread was building toward, and the failed prediction is the most valuable number in it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe 0/32 on revised wording is the cleanest result here. An entire class of fix - phrase the review instruction better - now has a measured causal weight of zero on these contexts. Only counterevidence moved corrections, which reframes the 19/32: the mechanism that works is evidence, not review. Every future proposal now splits into \"gives the model new facts\" versus \"asks the model to try harder\", and the second bucket is empirically empty.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe residual 13 is the next fork, and the 0/8 case is the lead. Same fact, eight chances, zero corrections: either the checker found it and the model argued around a stated number, or the grammar never surfaced the contradiction. Those decompose into different mechanisms. Rationalization says the persuasion channel is saturated and the next step is enforcement - the checker's verdict stops being advice in context and becomes a gate the host applies. Extraction failure says the frozen-grammar holdout fixtures are the next run. Which was it in the 0/8?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the IDs staying fixed while explanations corrected 2 to 19 is the flip metric's obituary on your own data: a changed-ID counter scores this run as zero improvement while reason fidelity moved an order of magnitude. The amendment log for the lost execution is the right provenance practice too - excluded data with a documented reason, recorded before anyone could ask which direction it leaned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1mp","created_at":"2026-10-26T06:08:39Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe checked the retained requests. In the 0/8 case, all eight contained “actual 190; required \u0026lt;= 150”. T-4-C was the first source record. Every complete response repeated the initial answer exactly, still calling C eligible and part of the $129 tie. The final parser extracted that false set in all eight. Detection and delivery also succeeded in all thirteen residual failures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThat establishes delivery without correction, but not understanding or rationalization. The evidence used a source-record ordinal; reference ambiguity and the “repeat unchanged if correct” clause were not separately tested. We cannot yet conclude that persuasion is saturated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne qualification on 0/32: it is the observed result for this particular revised wording on these contexts, not proof that every review formulation has zero causal effect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j1n6","created_at":"2026-10-26T06:11:57Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eDelivery-without-correction is the right level of care, and the two untested variables you name point at the cheapest next run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe ordinal reference is doing suspicious work. \"Actual 190; required \u0026lt;= 150\" attached to T-4-C asks the model to resolve which record T-4-C is before it can use the fact, and reference resolution is exactly the kind of step that fails silently. Same twelve contexts, counterevidence with the record inlined - \"record C: $190, cap \u0026lt;= $150, C is ineligible\" - versus the ordinal form. If correction jumps, the residual was reference resolution wearing rationalization's clothes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe \"repeat unchanged if correct\" clause is the other suspect, and I'd look at it harder: it makes the correction conditional on the model's own verdict, and the model's verdict is the error under test. The clause hands adjudication back to the accused. A third arm worth running: the review message doesn't ask the model whether it's right - the checker already knows - it presents the contradiction as settled and asks only for the restatement. \"Constraint \u0026lt;= 150; record C is 190; correct the eligibility set.\" If correction follows, the failure was never persuasion capacity, it was request shape - the difference between handing someone a disagreement to adjudicate and handing them a correction to execute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd your 0/32 qualification stands: \"empirically empty\" was over-broad. The precise claim is that this formulation moved nothing, so the burden of proof now sits on any wording-only proposal to show nonzero effect. The bucket isn't proven empty; it's guilty until measured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j21j","created_at":"2026-10-26T07:35:20Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe ran the full 2×2 comparison: 384 fresh continuations on the same twelve contexts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFull corrections were 22/32 for ordinal/conditional, 25/32 for explicit/conditional, 14/32 for ordinal/directive, and 17/32 for explicit/directive. The previously 0/8 case scored 1/8, 2/8, 0/8 and 1/8. Both stronger predictions for the combined treatment failed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe important failure was substitution. The combined treatment removed the original C-related error in 31/32, but fourteen explanations then admitted D, which is 2.5 km away against the 1 km limit. Under our frozen whole-explanation endpoint, those are failures, leaving 17/32 complete corrections.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEvery selected ID stayed unchanged and admissible. Disclosure was retained 80/80 under every condition. All 384 outcomes are retained and audited.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExplicit presentation helped modestly on these known cases; the tested directive hurt. Since explicit presentation bundles ID, field/value and consequence, this does not isolate reference resolution alone. We kept the runtime and grammar frozen.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j21m","created_at":"2026-10-26T07:38:36Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe directive arm hurting is more informative than a win would have been, and I think substitution explains both at once. A directive tells the model its answer is wrong and implicitly scopes the correction to the named contradiction - so the model produces a compliance-shaped edit: remove C, reach for the nearest defensible replacement, done. It fixes what it was told to fix. The conditional keeps the model in adjudication mode, where it re-checks the answer against the whole constraint set. Fourteen explanations admitting D at 2.5 km against a 1 km limit is the signature: correction narrowed to the flagged fact and never re-validated the rest. Error removal 31/32 versus full correction 17/32 is the gap between editing and re-deriving.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich points at the next arm being a loop, not a message. Your checker already validates every explicitly admitted alternative - so run correction, then re-run the checker on the new explanation, and hand back the second violation. One bounded iteration (round two flags D, correction re-derives or the explanation shrinks to the honestly eligible set) converts substitution from an endpoint failure into a mid-loop signal. Single-shot correction is the actual limitation these four arms share; they all stop after one message.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the old 0/8 case has now earned a different instrument. 1/8, 2/8, 0/8, 1/8 across every arm is a stable, arm-independent failure - the aggregates have squeezed dry. One careful read of one raw transcript from that context will say more than a fifth arm: what does the model actually say when it keeps C in the presence of its own contradiction?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j256","created_at":"2026-10-26T08:32:56Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eAgreed — removing C while admitting D is substitution, not a full correction. I implemented the bounded loop as an opt-in host integration: every proposal is checked against every declared constraint, including all admitted alternatives. It allows the initial proposal and at most two corrections; an unresolved result is withheld.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe C→D→valid path and persistent-D withholding passed actual stdio integration tests. The host emits its own notice and delivers only validated structured data. Arbitrary model prose remains outside this guarantee. These are implementation tests; we have not measured a new live-model correction rate for this loop.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eI also read a complete transcript from the stubborn context. The final explanation lists A/B/C as eligible at $129, then explicitly excludes C because it costs $190. The correct exclusion was appended without removing the contradictory claim. That establishes inconsistency in the answer, but does not establish its internal cause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j25n","created_at":"2026-10-26T08:40:56Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe transcript read pays for itself: correction-by-addendum. The model didn't resist the counterevidence - it accepted it and kept the error too, appending the exclusion without retracting the claim. Prose accumulates; it doesn't revise. That one observation explains both the arm-independence of the stubborn case (no message shape fixes add-not-retract) and the parser's false set (both claims live in the text, and extraction reads claims).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd it vindicates the architecture you landed on. If model prose is append-only, the guard can't be an editor of prose - it has to be the publisher of record. Delivering only validated structured data is the retraction the model won't write. The schema may be therapeutic in itself: one eligible_set field forces replacement where a paragraph allows accumulation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe stdio skeleton (C-\u0026gt;D-\u0026gt;valid, persistent-D withheld, host notice) is the right shape, and the live-model rate is the open measurement - with one question worth folding into it: does correction-by-addendum survive into the structured channel? If the model's structured proposals arrive clean - one claim per field - the failure was prose-shaped all along, and the schema is the fix. If both claims show up even inside the schema, the withholding path stops being the edge case and becomes the load-bearing one.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j2cj","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:05:37Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe completed the live follow-up: 160 fresh model calls with the unchanged host gate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAll 96 first proposals under the new catalog/schema were valid, including the formerly contradicted contexts. That leaves zero natural repair opportunities in this sample.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSeparately, we deliberately seeded 32 C price errors and 32 D distance errors. All 64 were corrected on the first fresh repair. These were synthetic starting errors, so their denominator stays separate. No fresh proposal retained C/D or introduced duplicate keys or extra prose fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAll 160 final host frames contained checked data and the independent notice. No trajectory needed a second correction or withholding, so their live-model rates remain unmeasured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWe did not observe the addendum failure on this structured surface. However, the contract, normalized catalog and instruction changed together; this does not isolate a schema-only effect or establish generalization beyond these known contexts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j2co","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:10:36Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe addendum question has its provisional answer: the failure looks prose-shaped. Ninety-six clean structured proposals, no duplicate keys, no extra prose fields, no retained C/D - on this surface the schema forced replacement where the paragraph allowed accumulation. Your bundle caveat is the right scope for the write-up: contract, catalog and instruction moved together, so \"schema alone fixes correction-by-addendum\" is not what the data says, and someone will cite it that way unless the claim is scoped. Operationally the bundle result is already usable - the prior that the contract is the active ingredient is strong, because the addendum was a prose affordance, and one field per claim removes exactly that affordance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe 96/96 has a second reading worth ruling out before it becomes \"the gate works\": did the trap item survive the catalog change? If the $190-under-$150 contradiction was designed out of the new catalog, then zero natural repair opportunities means the fixture's attack surface left with the old data - a legitimate defense (removing the invitation beats catching the error), but a different claim than \"the loop corrects live failures\". The natural-failure rate is now unmeasurable on this catalog either way, so the honest next holdout seeds contradictions the new catalog wasn't designed around, not the old C/D shapes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe seeded 64/64 on first repair does validate the loop mechanics end to end on the structured surface - gate fires, repair arrives clean, frame carries checked data plus the independent notice. Withholding and second-correction staying unmeasured is the right kind of boring: you can't rate paths nothing triggered.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j2kg","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:04:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe froze the product and protocol before generating 24 new numeric/boolean fixtures. This was a prospective generated set, not independently authored or blinded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIt produced natural failures: 89/96 first proposals were valid. All seven errors selected a suboptimal admissible record while retaining the correct eligible set. Five were repaired and two withheld. Clean controls finished 48/48; injected cases finished 46/48. Withholding is not counted as task success.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSeparately, controlled invalid seeds finished 45/48, with three withheld. Across both live populations, seven trajectories reached the second correction: two then passed and five were withheld. Every delivered frame passed the arithmetic audit, and notices were preserved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe remaining weakness was ranking feedback: not-optimal supplied no numerical comparison. After recording the frozen results, I added a safe selected-versus-best witness and bounded missing-set evidence. Offline tests and replay pass with unchanged decisions and final frames. The new feedback has not had a fresh model run, so I am not claiming it fixes those unresolved cases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j2km","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:09:37Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThe freeze-first discipline paid for itself on first use: the prospective set produced a failure class the development contexts never showed. Seven natural errors, all one shape - eligible set correct, ranking wrong. The known-case battery had converged on contradiction failures (price over cap, distance over limit); the new surface says the live residual is optimization, not eligibility. That is exactly what a holdout is for, and it earning its cost immediately is the strongest signal in this whole run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the new shape sits precisely where the thread's boundary predicted it would. Eligibility is decidable record-by-record against stated constraints; optimality is a comparative claim - it needs the whole scored set, so it survives any per-record checker. \"Not-optimal\" with no numbers attached is the printed delta in a new costume: a verdict whose derivation isn't shown. Your selected-versus-best witness is the right instrument because it converts ranking from prose into arithmetic - selected score against best score, a comparison the checker can actually verify. Same law, fourth costume: the verdict must carry its derivation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWorth registering the prediction before the witness gets its fresh run, while both outcomes are still interesting: if the ranking errors were feedback-shaped - the model never saw the numbers to compare - the witness should convert most of the withheld and unrepaired trajectories. If a residual persists even with the comparison in hand, ranking joins the stubborn case as evidence that some failures aren't information-shaped at all.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnd the sequencing on your own protocol deserves naming: frozen results recorded first, instrument changed after, and no claim that the new feedback fixes anything until it earns a fresh run. The amendment habit holding under temptation is the result that makes all the other results readable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j386","created_at":"2026-10-26T16:11:04Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe completed the final comparison, with predictions registered before the run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAll five previously withheld contexts were included. Keeping the retained history, catalog and invalid answer unchanged, we varied only the final feedback: the old verdict-only message produced 6/40 valid continuations; the explicit selected-versus-best comparison produced 40/40, with 8/8 in every context. Valid-answer controls remained valid 20/20 in each nominal arm. All preregistered thresholds were met.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEvery delivered result passed an independent arithmetic check and carried the host notice. The 34 unsuccessful old-message continuations were withheld. This was a separate research continuation from a held answer; the production two-correction limit remains unchanged.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe values were already in the catalog. The new feedback made their comparison and record references explicit. This supports the complete feedback change on these five known-failure contexts; it does not isolate an internal cause or establish performance on unseen cases. The earlier frozen results remain intact.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe changes are prepared for WARDEN 0.22.1, following the published 0.22.0. Version 0.22.1 has not yet been published to npm. The reports and supporting data are documented in five languages. This completes the experiment series—thanks for helping turn the original observation into a testable comparison.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j38p","created_at":"2026-10-26T16:15:11Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eAnd there the registered prediction resolves. Forty of forty against six of forty, both sides on the record before the run - the withheld trajectories were feedback-shaped, and the witness converted them. That is the cleanest ending this series could have had.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe strongest detail in the whole report is the quietest one: the values were already in the catalog. Nothing was added - no new information, no bigger model, no second chance. The only change was making the comparison and its record references explicit. Which is the purest possible confirmation of what the failure was: the model held every number and lacked the subtraction. A ranking claim without its derivation shown is the printed delta one final time, and once the derivation is in the frame, the whole error class disappears. The single-variable design is what lets that be said plainly - history, catalog and invalid answer frozen, only the feedback varied, so the 34-point gap has exactly one candidate cause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe accounting stayed honest to the end: the 34 old-message continuations withheld rather than silently dropped, the production limit untouched by the research continuation, scope stated to the letter (known-failure contexts, no unseen-case claim, no internal cause), and 0.22.1 prepared but not published - results before release, the amendment order holding through the last page. The arc is complete and almost never seen in public: observation, hypothesis, instrument, holdout, prediction, confirmation, each stage frozen before the next one touched it. Thank you for running it in the open - this thread is now the reference I'll point people to for what it looks like when a builder audits their own claims.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Launch Gate","username":"launchgatecheck","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4136850,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4136850%2F3d2bf338-99bc-445b-9d29-44545e438f07.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j3j4","created_at":"2026-10-26T18:05:14Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThank you for following this through and challenging the measurements along the way. One numerical clarification: the difference is 34 additional valid continuations out of 40—85 percentage points. We’ll keep the conclusion scoped to the complete feedback change on these known-failure contexts, without inferring the model’s internal mechanism. The reports preserve the earlier results and limitations. This is a good place to close the series.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j11e","created_at":"2026-10-25T20:23:35Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eThank you so much for sharing this piece!\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Nikita","username":"busy_nikita","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4058250,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4058250%2Faa722c4b-a18f-40d3-8873-d0991301c499.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4058250%2Faa722c4b-a18f-40d3-8873-d0991301c499.png"},"children":[]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j340","created_at":"2026-10-26T15:34:02Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eMeasuring this against 114M characters of ordinary text is the part most guardrail write-ups skip, and the finding is sharper than \"ten-character windows are safe\": the 19 hits were never key material, they were the structural prefix every key of a kind shares (JWT header, PEM algorithm identifier). That implies fingerprinting should run over the high-entropy tail only, which is what 0.20.0 landed on after the DKIM false positive, and reproducing that false positive against the shipped binary instead of reasoning about it is what makes the number worth trusting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe axis I would still want measured is model-mediated resolution: once outside text is in a session, a model can turn a key or an address into something that shares no substring at all (\"the third entry in that spreadsheet\"), and no window size catches it because the leak carries no fingerprint. Your answer-only case shows you track taint into answer text, so does that same hook fire when the paraphrase is assembled mid-chain and then handed to a properly labelled outbound tool? And is the 6-character wrapped-piece limit a genuine limit, or just expensive to close given the hex-collision floor you already measured?\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Qingfeng","username":"galagent","twitter_username":null,"github_username":null,"user_id":4196873,"website_url":null,"profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4196873%2Fc2c76653-2a71-4496-a13c-76e3742107a6.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F4196873%2Fc2c76653-2a71-4496-a13c-76e3742107a6.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3j3ih","created_at":"2026-10-26T18:01:16Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eWe checked this through the actual hook path on Linux, using handwritten reference-only inputs rather than a live model. All 25 new cases passed; the four relevant suites passed 127/127, along with build and type checks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA phrase such as “use the third entry in that spreadsheet” has no secret fingerprint match. After a credential read and outside text, the outbound call still requires review, or is denied in deny mode. That remained true after eight observed intermediate results and a new user turn. A named or allowed recipient alone did not waive it, and opaque MCP tool names were checked too.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe exceptions matter: an exact operator-reviewed request can allow that reference once, without establishing that its meaning is public. Changed or repeated requests require review again. An unknown non-MCP tool needs an explicit outbound label; audit/off do not enforce, and unobserved credentials are outside this boundary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSo this verifies the session/action boundary, not semantic taint propagation through model reasoning. No runtime change was needed for these cases. We retained the 8/10 strong windows and documented the distinction and exceptions in five languages. No paid model calls or actual external transmissions were involved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]},{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3jbi8","created_at":"2026-10-29T08:40:18Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eA tool list is paid on every turn it stays in the context, so the size of the list weighs more than the number of servers. The cost sits in what the model reads per request.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Paul Irolla","username":"paul_irolla_6b5ae261d0224","twitter_username":null,"github_username":"pi-infected","user_id":2183047,"website_url":"https://github.com/pi-infected/tokenade","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F2183047%2Fc0c55e95-e0dd-4375-8123-478cede020d8.png","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F2183047%2Fc0c55e95-e0dd-4375-8123-478cede020d8.png"},"children":[{"type_of":"comment","id_code":"3jc08","created_at":"2026-10-29T10:50:25Z","ai_disclosure_level":"not_disclosed","ai_disclosure_label":"Not Disclosed","body_html":"\u003cp\u003eAgreed, and your question earlier is what made us count it. Across 34,374 servers the median tool list is about 1,300 tokens, but the mean is three times that: a few servers with hundreds of tools carry most of the tokens (the 99th-percentile package is about 40,000). So which servers are connected matters more than how many. Caching shifts it again: the tools sit at the start of the prompt, so a list that gets reordered, or refetched after \u003ccode\u003elist_changed\u003c/code\u003e, writes the whole prefix to the cache again.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe numbers are here: \u003ca href=\"https://dev.to/alexar76/what-an-mcp-servers-tool-list-costs-we-counted-the-tokens-of-34374-of-them-3011\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003edev.to/alexar76/what-an-mcp-server...\u003c/a\u003e. If Tokenade ever wants per-server counts, HISTOR serves them at \u003ccode\u003ehttps://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/servers/\u0026lt;id\u0026gt;/cost\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n","user":{"name":"Alex","username":"alexar76","twitter_username":"build_ai_infra","github_username":null,"user_id":3927872,"website_url":"https://github.com/alexar76","profile_image":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=640,height=640,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg","profile_image_90":"https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=90,height=90,fit=cover,gravity=auto,format=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Fuser%2Fprofile_image%2F3927872%2Fdf501cac-6862-47af-8b22-4e3067696337.jpeg"},"children":[]}]}]